/* Notes for s40 — generated by tools/build-notes.js from content/notes. Do not edit by hand. */
PG.notes("l344", "<p class=\"summary\">Sets up a fresh dfx project called dkeeper and swaps its default plain HTML/JS frontend for the React Keeper app built earlier in the course. It is a follow-along checklist of file moves, one rename, a webpack edit, a tsconfig and a package.json replacement, ending with the React UI running locally with no backend yet.</p>\n<h3>Why React on the Internet Computer</h3>\n<ul><li>A DApp frontend is not limited to hand-written HTML/CSS/JS; the frontend canister can serve any bundled web app, and most Web3 frontends use React.</li><li>The module reuses the React-only Keeper app (a Google Keep style note taker with title + content). On its own it has no backend, so notes vanish on refresh.</li><li>Goal of the section: add a Motoko backend canister so notes are stored and survive reloads, on-chain.</li><li>The lecture assumes the React section of the course is done; the steps are many and order matters, so the suggested way to work is watch a step, pause, repeat it yourself.</li></ul>\n<h3>Create the project</h3>\n<ul><li>From the folder that holds your ICP projects, run dfx new dkeeper. Keeping the exact name dkeeper matters because later lessons refer to canister and declaration names derived from it (dkeeper_backend etc. in current dfx, dkeeper / dkeeper_assets in the older template shown in the video).</li><li>Wait for the Dfinity logo, which signals scaffolding finished, then open the folder in VS Code.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Newer dfx templates ask you to choose backend language and frontend framework and generate names like dkeeper_backend and dkeeper_frontend; the reference repo uses these and a Vite frontend instead of webpack.</li></ul>\n<h3>Swap the default frontend for the React app (older webpack template)</h3>\n<ul><li>In src there are two canister folders: the Motoko one (main.mo, left untouched for now) and the assets one containing src/index.html, index.js and assets/main.css. Delete those three default frontend files.</li><li>Download the finished Keeper project from the course resources and extract it. Its public folder has index.html and styles.css; its src folder has the components folder and index.js.</li><li>Move index.html, the components folder and index.js into the project's frontend src folder. Move styles.css into the frontend assets folder.</li><li>Rename index.js to index.jsx. This is the conventional extension for files containing JSX and it is what lets the bundler's loader pick the file up as React code.</li><li>Open webpack.config.js, find module.exports and the entry setting, and change the entry file name from index.js to index.jsx (line number varies between template versions, so search for it).</li><li>Further down the config, a commented block is labelled as the section to enable when using React. Uncomment it (Cmd+/ on Mac, Ctrl+/ on Windows). It wires in the TypeScript loader that compiles the JSX during the build.</li><li>Save, then add the provided tsconfig.json from the course resources next to webpack.config.js. It holds compiler options such as the JavaScript target version and JSX handling.</li><li>Open package.json, delete all contents and paste in the contents of the provided package.txt. This supplies React, ReactDOM, Material UI icon packages and the loader tooling the build needs.</li></ul>\n<h3>Run it locally</h3>\n<ul><li>Terminal 1: dfx start launches the simulated local ICP replica (leave it running).</li><li>Terminal 2: npm install pulls the dependencies listed in package.json. Deprecation warnings (mostly from the icon library) can be ignored; upgrading versions risks cascading dependency conflicts.</li><li>dfx deploy builds and installs the canisters on the local replica.</li><li>npm start runs the dev server; open localhost:8080 to see the React Keeper UI.</li><li>Adding notes works and icons render, which proves the frontend wiring is correct. Refreshing wipes the notes because no Motoko code or storage is involved yet. The next lesson connects main.mo.</li></ul>\n<h3>What the reference repo looks like (Vite version)</h3>\n<ul><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo is a modern dfx scaffold: src/dkeeper_frontend holds index.html, vite.config.js, tsconfig.json, public/styles.css and src/main.jsx plus App.jsx and the four components. dfx.json declares dkeeper_backend (motoko) and dkeeper_frontend (type assets, source dist, workspace dkeeper_frontend).</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> index.html lives at the frontend root and loads /src/main.jsx via a module script tag; main.jsx uses createRoot(...).render with StrictMode around App.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> vite.config.js proxies /api to the local replica on port 4943, loads CANISTER_ and DFX_ environment variables, and adds an alias to the generated declarations folder. The dev server runs on port 3000 (npm start), not 8080.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Because Vite handles JSX natively, the webpack entry edit, TypeScript-loader uncommenting and package.txt steps are not needed there; the Keeper files are simply copied in.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Command sequence for the whole lesson</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">dfx new dkeeper          # scaffold project\ncd dkeeper\n# ...move Keeper files, rename index.js -&gt; index.jsx, edit webpack.config.js, add tsconfig.json, replace package.json...\ndfx start                # terminal 1: local replica\nnpm install              # terminal 2\ndfx deploy\nnpm start                # then open localhost:8080</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">webpack.config.js entry change (older template)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">module.exports = {\n  target: &quot;web&quot;,\n  entry: {\n    index: path.join(__dirname, &quot;src&quot;, &quot;dkeeper_assets&quot;, &quot;src&quot;, &quot;index.jsx&quot;),\n  },\n  // ...\n};</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Reference repo entry point (Vite): main.jsx</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import { StrictMode } from &quot;react&quot;;\nimport { createRoot } from &quot;react-dom/client&quot;;\nimport App from &quot;./App.jsx&quot;;\n\ncreateRoot(document.getElementById(&quot;root&quot;)).render(\n  &lt;StrictMode&gt;\n    &lt;App /&gt;\n  &lt;/StrictMode&gt;\n);</pre>\n<h2>The task: Port the Keeper React app into a dfx project</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> A brand new dfx new dkeeper project plus the finished Keeper app zip and the tsconfig.json / package.txt downloads from the course resources.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Get the React Keeper UI served by the local dfx setup, without any backend logic yet.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Run dfx new dkeeper and open it in VS Code.</li><li>Delete the default frontend index.html, index.js and CSS file.</li><li>Copy index.html, components and index.js into the frontend src folder; copy styles.css into the frontend assets folder.</li><li>Rename index.js to index.jsx and update the webpack entry to match.</li><li>Uncomment the React/TypeScript loader block in webpack.config.js.</li><li>Add tsconfig.json beside webpack.config.js.</li><li>Replace package.json contents with the supplied package.txt.</li><li>Run dfx start, npm install, dfx deploy, npm start and open localhost:8080 (port 3000 in the Vite reference repo).</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> The Keeper app renders and lets you add notes with working icons; refreshing clears them.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting to rename index.js to index.jsx or to update the webpack entry to match.</li><li>Leaving the React loader block in webpack.config.js commented out, so JSX fails to compile.</li><li>Skipping the package.json replacement, which leaves React and icon libraries uninstalled.</li><li>Running npm start before dfx deploy.</li><li>Chasing deprecation warnings by upgrading packages, which causes dependency conflicts.</li><li>Naming the project differently from dkeeper, which breaks later import names.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo's index.html contains a stray 'font-family' fragment inside the link tag; remove it.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l345", "<p class=\"summary\">Writes the first real Motoko backend for the Keeper app: a Note record type, a List of notes held in a variable, and a createNote function that prepends a note. Then App.jsx is changed to import the generated canister declarations and call createNote whenever a note is added, with Debug.print used to confirm it arrived. This completes the C of CRUD.</p>\n<h3>Before starting</h3>\n<ul><li>The React frontend from the previous lesson must already run locally; if it does not, redo that lesson first because nothing here fixes frontend setup problems.</li><li>Open main.mo and remove the template hello-world code. The actor is rebuilt from scratch and named DKeeper (the D prefix is the instructor's naming habit; use the same names to avoid command mismatches).</li></ul>\n<h3>Defining a Note type in Motoko</h3>\n<ul><li>Declare a custom type with the type keyword, a name, an equals sign and curly braces holding fields: type Note = { title : Text; content : Text; }.</li><li>Inside a type declaration, fields use a colon to give their type and are separated by semicolons. Text is Motoko's string type.</li><li>The type describes the shape of the data stored for each note, much like deciding which keys a JavaScript note object has.</li><li>The lecture suggests marking the type public so the JavaScript side can see it. (Extra) In the reference repo the type is not marked public, yet it still works, because the generated candid interface exposes the types that public functions use.</li></ul>\n<h3>Holding notes in a List</h3>\n<ul><li>Create a variable named notes of type List.List&lt;Note&gt;, initialised to an empty list with List.nil&lt;Note&gt;().</li><li>A List in Motoko is a linked list from the base library, similar in purpose to a JavaScript array but with different operations. The element type goes in angle brackets.</li><li>Using List.something means using the module's functions, so the module must be imported: import List &quot;mo:base/List&quot;. The 'unbound variable List' error disappears after the import.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo declares the actor as persistent actor DKeeper, a newer Motoko form where state persists across upgrades by default, which matters in the later persistence lesson.</li></ul>\n<h3>The createNote function</h3>\n<ul><li>public func createNote(titleText : Text, contentText : Text) is callable from outside the canister. The JavaScript caller supplies two strings, in this order: title then content.</li><li>Inside, build the record: let newNote : Note = { title = titleText; content = contentText; }. Note the difference: type declarations use a colon between name and type, while record values use an equals sign.</li><li>List.push(newNote, notes) takes the item first and the list second and returns a brand new list with the item added at the front (prepend). It does not alter the original, unlike appending to the end of a JS array.</li><li>Because push returns a new list, assign it back with the reassignment operator: notes := List.push(newNote, notes).</li><li>Prepending matters later, because the order of notes on screen is affected by whether new entries go to the front or back.</li><li>For debugging, import Debug from mo:base/Debug and call Debug.print(debug_show(notes)). debug_show converts any value to printable text; the output appears in the terminal running dfx start.</li></ul>\n<h3>CRUD framing</h3>\n<ul><li>This is the Create step of CRUD (Create, Read, Update, Delete), the same idea as inserting documents into MongoDB in earlier backend modules. Read arrives in the next lesson.</li></ul>\n<h3>Calling the canister from App.jsx</h3>\n<ul><li>App.jsx has addNote, which the CreateArea component triggers when the user clicks Add. It calls setNotes with an updater that returns the previous notes plus the new one.</li><li>To reach the backend, import the canister object from the generated declarations folder, using a relative path that climbs up out of the frontend src folder (each ../ goes up one level): import { dkeeper_backend } from &quot;../../declarations/dkeeper_backend&quot; in the reference repo. The canister name comes from dfx.json.</li><li>The declarations are generated, so after changing main.mo run dfx deploy; the new createNote then appears in the generated interface (two text inputs, no return value).</li><li>In addNote, before returning, call dkeeper_backend.createNote(newNote.title, newNote.content). Argument order must match the Motoko parameter order, and both must be strings.</li><li>The attribute names title and content come from how CreateArea structures a note on the frontend, kept identical on both sides for consistency.</li><li>Expected behavior: on clicking Add the UI updates instantly through React state, and a print appears in the dfx start terminal showing the notes list with the typed title and content.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">main.mo after this lesson (reference repo version)</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">import List &quot;mo:base/List&quot;;\nimport Debug &quot;mo:base/Debug&quot;;\n\npersistent actor DKeeper {\n  type Note = {\n    title : Text;\n    content : Text;\n  };\n\n  var notes : List.List&lt;Note&gt; = List.nil&lt;Note&gt;();\n\n  public func createNote(titleText : Text, contentText : Text) {\n    let newNote : Note = {\n      title = titleText;\n      content = contentText;\n    };\n\n    notes := List.push(newNote, notes);\n    Debug.print(debug_show (notes));\n  };\n};</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">App.jsx changes: import the canister and call it inside addNote</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import { dkeeper_backend } from &quot;../../declarations/dkeeper_backend&quot;;\n\nfunction App() {\n  const [notes, setNotes] = useState([]);\n\n  function addNote(newNote) {\n    setNotes(prevNotes =&gt; {\n      dkeeper_backend.createNote(newNote.title, newNote.content);\n      return [...prevNotes, newNote];\n    });\n  }\n  // deleteNote and JSX unchanged\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: Store a note on the canister</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> dkeeper project from the previous lesson with the Keeper React UI running and default hello-world main.mo.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Each note added in the UI is also created in the Motoko canister and logged in the replica terminal.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Clear main.mo and import List and Debug from mo:base.</li><li>Create actor DKeeper with a Note type holding title and content as Text.</li><li>Add var notes : List.List&lt;Note&gt; initialised with List.nil&lt;Note&gt;().</li><li>Write public func createNote(titleText, contentText) building a Note and prepending it with List.push, assigning back with :=.</li><li>Print the list with Debug.print(debug_show(notes)).</li><li>In App.jsx import the canister from the declarations folder.</li><li>Call createNote(newNote.title, newNote.content) in addNote.</li><li>Run dfx deploy, refresh the app, add a note and check the dfx start terminal output.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> The note appears in the UI and the terminal prints the list with the same title and content.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">public func createNote(titleText : Text, contentText : Text) {\n  let newNote : Note = {\n    title = titleText;\n    content = contentText;\n  };\n  notes := List.push(newNote, notes);\n  Debug.print(debug_show (notes));\n};</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Mixing up colon (type declarations) and equals (record values).</li><li>Forgetting to import List, giving an unbound variable error.</li><li>Calling List.push without assigning the result back; the original list is unchanged.</li><li>Skipping dfx deploy so the declarations lack createNote.</li><li>Passing arguments in the wrong order, which swaps title and content.</li><li>Wrong relative path to the declarations folder.</li><li>Notes still disappear on refresh because the frontend does not read them back yet.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l346", "<p class=\"summary\">Adds the Read step of CRUD. A query function readNotes converts the stored List to an array and returns it; in App.jsx a useEffect with an empty dependency array calls an async fetchData on first load and puts the result in state. Also fixes the display order by prepending new notes in the frontend to match the backend.</p>\n<h3>State of play</h3>\n<ul><li>Notes created in the UI already reach the canister's notes list (visible in the debug output), but nothing reads them back, so the UI loses them on refresh.</li></ul>\n<h3>The readNotes query function</h3>\n<ul><li>Declare it as public query func readNotes() : async [Note]. It takes no input.</li><li>The query keyword marks a function that only reads state. Query calls skip consensus and respond much faster than update calls, so use them for any read-only method.</li><li>Return type is an array of Note, written [Note], not a List. Arrays map closely to JavaScript arrays, so the data arrives on the frontend as a plain array of objects with title and content.</li><li>The backend still stores a List because pushing and later removing items is cheaper than on an array (arrays are serialised and expensive to rebuild on chain). Convert only when returning.</li><li>List.toArray(notes) takes one argument, the list, and returns an array. Motoko's base library has fewer utility functions than JS or Python, so finding the right one means reading the module docs.</li><li>After editing main.mo, run dfx deploy so the generated declarations include readNotes.</li></ul>\n<h3>Loading data on first render with useEffect</h3>\n<ul><li>useState re-renders the component on updates, but a hook is needed to run code when the page first loads. useEffect runs after a component renders, including the first render.</li><li>First argument: a function to run (arrow function, no inputs). A console.log inside is a quick way to confirm when it fires.</li><li>The function passed to useEffect cannot itself be async, so define a separate async function fetchData and call it from inside the effect.</li><li>fetchData: const notesArray = await dkeeper_backend.readNotes(); then setNotes(notesArray). The await is needed because canister calls return promises.</li><li>Trap: setNotes changes state, which re-renders App, which triggers useEffect again, which fetches again, endlessly. The console shows the log repeating.</li><li>Fix: pass a second argument, a dependency array. useEffect re-runs only when a listed value changes; an empty array [] means run once, after the first render.</li><li>Result: add notes, refresh, and they stay, because they are read from the canister each load.</li></ul>\n<h3>Fixing the order</h3>\n<ul><li>Backend push prepends, so after a refresh the newest note appears first, but the frontend's addNote appended new notes to the end, so the order flipped on reload.</li><li>Newest-first matches how note apps normally behave and keeps new items visible at the top of a long list.</li><li>Change addNote's returned array from [...prevNotes, newNote] to [newNote, ...prevNotes]. Now the UI order matches the canister order before and after refresh.</li></ul>\n<h3>Remaining gap</h3>\n<ul><li>Delete only changes frontend state, so a refreshed page re-fetches the deleted note from the canister. Handled in the next lesson.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Motoko: the new query function (added below createNote)</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">public query func readNotes() : async [Note] {\n  return List.toArray(notes);\n};</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">App.jsx after this lesson (reference repo)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import { useEffect, useState } from &quot;react&quot;;\nimport { dkeeper_backend } from &quot;../../declarations/dkeeper_backend&quot;;\n\nfunction App() {\n  const [notes, setNotes] = useState([]);\n\n  function addNote(newNote) {\n    setNotes((prevNotes) =&gt; {\n      dkeeper_backend.createNote(newNote.title, newNote.content);\n      return [newNote, ...prevNotes];\n    });\n  }\n\n  useEffect(() =&gt; {\n    fetchData();\n  }, []);\n\n  async function fetchData() {\n    const notesArray = await dkeeper_backend.readNotes();\n    setNotes(notesArray);\n  }\n  // deleteNote and JSX unchanged\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: Read notes back from the canister</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Project from the previous lesson where createNote stores notes in a List but the UI never loads them.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Notes persist across page refreshes and appear newest first.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Add public query func readNotes() : async [Note] returning List.toArray(notes).</li><li>Run dfx deploy.</li><li>In App.jsx import useEffect.</li><li>Add useEffect(() =&gt; { fetchData(); }, []).</li><li>Write async function fetchData that awaits dkeeper_backend.readNotes() and calls setNotes.</li><li>Observe (optionally) the infinite loop by removing the [] and then restore it.</li><li>Change addNote to return [newNote, ...prevNotes].</li><li>Add notes, refresh and confirm they persist in the same order.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Refreshing keeps all notes, with the latest at the top before and after reload.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">useEffect(() =&gt; {\n  fetchData();\n}, []);\n\nasync function fetchData() {\n  const notesArray = await dkeeper_backend.readNotes();\n  setNotes(notesArray);\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Omitting the [] dependency array, which creates an endless fetch/render loop.</li><li>Making the useEffect callback itself async.</li><li>Forgetting await on the canister call, so a Promise is stored in state.</li><li>Returning the List directly instead of converting to an array.</li><li>Not redeploying after editing main.mo.</li><li>Appending on the frontend while the backend prepends, flipping the order on refresh.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> In React StrictMode dev builds effects run twice on mount; that is not the infinite loop.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l347", "<p class=\"summary\">Completes the Keeper backend with a removeNote function, built by combining List.take, List.drop and List.append to remove an element at an index, then wired into deleteNote in App.jsx. The lecture first poses this as a challenge using a Motoko playground to explore the list functions, then walks through the solution and finally shows how the stable keyword keeps data across canister upgrades.</p>\n<h3>The problem</h3>\n<ul><li>deleteNote in App.jsx only filters the frontend state array. After a refresh, fetchData reloads from the canister and the deleted note returns, so the canister must delete it too.</li><li>The Motoko List module available at the time had no remove-at-index helper, so it has to be assembled from smaller functions.</li><li>The Motoko function receives the note's index as a natural number: public func removeNote(id : Nat). Nat is a whole number from 0 up, which suits an array index.</li></ul>\n<h3>How take, drop and append behave</h3>\n<ul><li>List.take(list, n) returns a list with the first n elements. If the list is shorter than n, it returns a copy of the whole list.</li><li>List.drop(list, n) returns the list without its first n elements.</li><li>List.append(a, b) joins two lists, a first then b.</li><li>Remember that push prepends. In a playground that pushes A, B, C, D in order, the list reads D, C, B, A. Indices count from the front, so D is 0, C is 1, B is 2, A is 3.</li><li>Candid UI in the Motoko playground shows a List as deeply nested pairs, not a flat array, which is why readNotes converts to an array before sending to JS.</li><li>To remove B (index 2): take(list, 2) gives D, C; drop(list, 2 + 1) gives A; append them to get D, C, A.</li><li>Playground exercise suggested: deploy the sample with drop, take and getNotes methods, try different numbers, and note that upgrading resets a non-stable variable.</li></ul>\n<h3>The solution in Motoko</h3>\n<ul><li>let listFront = List.take(notes, id) keeps everything before the target.</li><li>let listBack = List.drop(notes, id + 1) skips the target and keeps everything after it.</li><li>notes := List.append(listFront, listBack) reassigns the state to the joined list. Two locals and the assignment can be collapsed into one line, but separate steps are easier to follow.</li><li>Lists are immutable values: each function returns a new list, so the final := assignment is what actually changes the stored notes.</li></ul>\n<h3>Frontend wiring</h3>\n<ul><li>In deleteNote, keep the existing setNotes filter so the UI changes immediately, and also call dkeeper_backend.removeNote(id) so the canister matches.</li><li>The id given to deleteNote is the array index of the note, passed from Note via props.onDelete(props.id). Because the frontend array and canister list are in the same newest-first order, the same index works on both sides.</li><li>Redeploy with dfx deploy before testing. Test: add two notes, delete one (disappears at once), refresh, and it stays gone because fetchData reads the already-trimmed list.</li></ul>\n<h3>Persistence across upgrades</h3>\n<ul><li>When main.mo changes and dfx deploy upgrades the canister, ordinary variables are reset, so the notes list empties. A deploy with unchanged code does not upgrade.</li><li>Marking the variable stable (stable var notes : List.List&lt;Note&gt; = List.nil&lt;Note&gt;()) makes it survive upgrades, behaving like a built-in database without any extra storage code.</li><li>Check: change the file (e.g. add or remove a Debug.print), deploy again, refresh the app, and notes remain.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo declares persistent actor DKeeper and plain var notes. In that newer Motoko form every var is persistent by default, so the stable keyword is not written. In older code with a plain actor, add stable explicitly.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Stable variables have type restrictions, and large or changing types need migration planning; the simple Note record in a List is fine.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Final main.mo (reference repo)</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">import List &quot;mo:base/List&quot;;\nimport Debug &quot;mo:base/Debug&quot;;\n\npersistent actor DKeeper {\n  type Note = {\n    title : Text;\n    content : Text;\n  };\n\n  var notes : List.List&lt;Note&gt; = List.nil&lt;Note&gt;();\n\n  public func createNote(titleText : Text, contentText : Text) {\n    let newNote : Note = {\n      title = titleText;\n      content = contentText;\n    };\n    notes := List.push(newNote, notes);\n    Debug.print(debug_show (notes));\n  };\n\n  public query func readNotes() : async [Note] {\n    return List.toArray(notes);\n  };\n\n  public func removeNote(id : Nat) {\n    let listFront = List.take(notes, id);\n    let listBack = List.drop(notes, id + 1);\n    notes := List.append(listFront, listBack);\n  };\n};</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">App.jsx deleteNote with backend call</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">function deleteNote(id) {\n  dkeeper_backend.removeNote(id);\n  setNotes((prevNotes) =&gt; {\n    return prevNotes.filter((noteItem, index) =&gt; {\n      return index !== id;\n    });\n  });\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Older-style actor with stable storage (as taught in the video)</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">actor DKeeper {\n  stable var notes : List.List&lt;Note&gt; = List.nil&lt;Note&gt;();\n  // ...functions as above\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Worked trace: remove index 2 from [D, C, B, A]</p><pre data-lang=\"txt\">take(list, 2)      -&gt; [D, C]\ndrop(list, 2 + 1)  -&gt; [A]\nappend(front, back) -&gt; [D, C, A]</pre>\n<h2>The task: Challenge: delete a note and keep it deleted</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> dkeeper with createNote and readNotes working; deleteNote only updates React state.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Deleting a note removes it from the canister so it does not reappear on refresh, and notes survive canister upgrades.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Try the sample Motoko playground linked in the course resources to see how take, drop and append behave on a list.</li><li>In main.mo write public func removeNote(id : Nat).</li><li>Inside, compute listFront with List.take(notes, id).</li><li>Compute listBack with List.drop(notes, id + 1).</li><li>Reassign notes := List.append(listFront, listBack).</li><li>In App.jsx deleteNote call dkeeper_backend.removeNote(id) in addition to the existing setNotes filter.</li><li>Run dfx deploy, add two notes, delete one, refresh and confirm it stays removed.</li><li>Make the notes variable stable (or use persistent actor), change the code, redeploy and confirm notes survive.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Deleted notes stay deleted after a refresh, and all notes remain after redeploying changed code.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"mo\">public func removeNote(id : Nat) {\n  let listFront = List.take(notes, id);\n  let listBack = List.drop(notes, id + 1);\n  notes := List.append(listFront, listBack);\n};</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting the + 1 in drop, which keeps the item you meant to delete.</li><li>Forgetting the := assignment, since List functions return new lists.</li><li>Mixing up index direction: push prepends, so index 0 is the newest note.</li><li>Frontend and canister orders disagreeing, so the wrong note gets removed (the fix from the previous lesson keeps them aligned).</li><li>Expecting data to survive upgrades without stable or a persistent actor.</li><li>Not redeploying after editing main.mo.</li><li>Note: removeNote has no bounds check; an id past the end simply leaves the list unchanged.</li></ul>");
